--- body ---

## Context

Author, 2026-09-16, after a night in which the same defect surfaced three times
in three disguises: "Je veux les états clairs, et les conditions de transition
aussi."

The framing he gave it is the right one — code proof, critical infrastructure,
Ada and Rust. Judged by that standard `state.phase` fails on the first
criterion: **its alphabet is not declared anywhere, and it is not closed.**
Two assignment sites copy in, verbatim, whatever string `host.blocked()`
returned. The scheduler names seven phases and then stores an open set of
others it has never enumerated.

Three consequences, all observed on 2026-09-15, all the same missing
exhaustiveness check:

- `SDT_PHASE_LABELS` carries fourteen entries, `SDT_BLOCKED_PHASES` five, and
  they are maintained in parallel with nothing linking them. `native-worker-busy`
  sits in the first and not the second — correct, as it happens, since it is a
  wait and not a block, but nothing in the code says so and ticket 0797's body
  filed it wrongly on exactly that reading.
- Ticket 0797's state table has to special-case the sync phase **by literal
  name**, because membership in `SDT_BLOCKED_PHASES` maps to a different row.
- `tests/sdt_sitter_scheduler.mjs` discovers the phase set **by regular
  expression over the source**, with a `phases.size >= 12` floor. Auditing by
  scraping is what a codebase does when it has no declaration to read.

## What this ticket builds

### 1. One declaration, and everything derives from it

A single frozen table — the alphabet — where each phase carries what is today
spread across two tables and several `if` chains: its **axis** (activity,
reason, intent, outcome), its **cadence class**, its **label id** (or the
deliberate `null` that `ready` and `waiting` carry), and whether it is
**terminal**.

`SDT_PHASE_LABELS` and `SDT_BLOCKED_PHASES` stop being hand-maintained lists
and become projections of it. That alone makes the `native-worker-busy` class
of misfiling impossible rather than merely absent.

### 1b. Two axes whose PRODUCT is the state, not a hierarchy

Outer — **is work being admitted?** `RUNNING`, `PAUSED`, `DEFERRED`, `ERROR`,
`ENDING`, `VANISHED`. Six.

Inner — **what is in flight?** `census`, `draining`, `extracting`, `idle`. Four.

**They are orthogonal, and the proof is in the code's own behaviour:
`extracting` stays true under `DEFERRED`.** A sync beginning mid-document
leaves the sitter deferred on the outside and extracting on the inside. If the
inner value were a *child* of `RUNNING` it would be undefined there; it is not.
So the full state is the product of two axes, each varying independently, and
these combinations are all meaningful: `DEFERRED × extracting` (held, a
document finishing), `PAUSED × extracting` (0797's "checked, a document still
in flight" row), `VANISHED × extracting` (removed mid-document), `RUNNING ×
idle`.

**Do not unfold the product into a flat set.** Twenty-four names would lose the
independence of the axes and make the alphabet larger rather than smaller,
which is the error the code already makes in miniature.

Not every cell is reachable — `ERROR × extracting` probably is not, `ENDING ×
census` probably is. **Enumerating which cells exist is the transition work,
not a preliminary to it.**

### 1c. `DEFERRED` carries a SET of reasons, and the open set leaves the alphabet

`low-disk`, `cpu-busy`, `low-memory`, `storage-unavailable` and
`resources-unavailable` are not states. They are the reason carried by
`DEFERRED`. The vocabulary the host may invent becomes a payload, so the
scheduler is exhaustive over its own states without ever learning
`bootstrap.js`'s words — which dissolves the decorrelated review's objection to
a transition graph at the root rather than by argument.

**Several can hold at once, and today the code cannot say so.** `blocked()`
returns one string: the first condition that matched, in its own evaluation
order. If the disk is low and the load is high and a sync is running, the
reader sees whichever was tested first. That manufactures phantom transitions —
when the first condition lifts, the phase appears to change to a reason that
was true all along, and nothing actually moved. A reason *set* makes a real
transition precise: the outer state changed, or the set changed.

**The cost, named rather than discovered later:** `blocked()` short-circuits
today, returning on the first refusal and so skipping the expensive procfs and
storage reads. Computing the full set pays all of them every time. Tolerable
once per admission; not at an arbitrary cadence.

### 1d. A correction, recorded so it is not restored by someone who thinks it was forgotten

An earlier revision of this ticket separated `HELD` from `BLOCKED` on the
criterion *cannot* versus *could but should not*. **That criterion is wrong.**
Checked reason by reason: `cpu-busy` is a load average above the core count —
indexing is possible, it would merely slow the machine. `low-memory` and
`low-disk` are the same shape. Only `storage-unavailable` is a true cannot, the
attachment's directory being absent. And `resources-unavailable` is neither: it
is "could not measure", which the code refuses out of prudence. So the
criterion isolates one reason and files `cpu-busy` with sync — it does not
separate the two.

What actually separates them is **not a state distinction at all**: it is
whether the condition is legible to the user as an event with a beginning and
an end. A sync is visible in Zotero's own interface and resolves itself;
`cpu-busy` is a sample that may clear in seconds and whose display flickers.

So each reason declares a **presentation class**, and 0797's checkbox reads
that field instead of special-casing a phase by literal name — which was the
exact wart 0797 had to carry. The wart disappears rather than being documented.

### 1e. The declaration's fields

Each outer state, each inner state, and each reason carries: its **axis**, its
**cadence class**, its **label id** (or the deliberate `null` that `ready` and
`waiting` hold), its **terminality**, and for reasons its **presentation
class**. `SDT_PHASE_LABELS` and `SDT_BLOCKED_PHASES` become projections rather
than parallel hand-maintained lists — which is what makes the
`native-worker-busy` class of misfiling impossible instead of merely absent.

### 1f. The evidence that this is description, not invention

The code has already been forced to grow the second axis twice, each time
because a fact would not fit the flat variable: `removalNoticed`, a separate
boolean that overrides the phase at render, and `switched-off`, which occupies a
phase slot while `toggleSDTSwitch` reads it as the switch's polarity — control
state wearing a phase's clothes.

Two states verified in source rather than assumed. **`ENDING` is a teardown
transient**: `blocked()` returns `'disabled'` when `!alive`, and `shutdown()`
clears `alive`, so the value exists only between teardown starting and the pump
noticing at its next gate — nothing sits in it. **`VANISHED` already exists
outside the alphabet**, as `removalNoticed`, set by `checkSDTStillInstalled()`.

**What it costs** is the one-way door named below: the journal's `phase` goes
from one field to two plus a reason set, and every incident JSON under
`verification/incidents/` is read against the old shape.

### 2. A total `setPhase`

Every assignment goes through one function, and that function is **total**: a
value outside the alphabet is not written silently. It is refused, and the
attempt is emitted as a defect record so that "the host said a word we do not
know" becomes an observable event instead of a silent state. This is a range
check at the point of assignment, in the Ada sense, and it is the whole reason
the funnel is worth building — not `phaseSince`, which is a separate matter and
is addressed below under what this ticket does NOT claim.

### 3. The transition conditions, declared

The author asked for these explicitly, and they are the part with the real
design risk. A `from × to` relation, each edge carrying the condition that
licenses it, checked by `setPhase` and enumerable by a test.

The eleven assignment sites today, cited by symbol because the line numbers
moved three times on 2026-09-15 alone — in `scheduler.js`'s `pump()` unless
noted:

| from | to | what licenses it |
|---|---|---|
| `switched-off` | `ready` | the loop re-entered after a `start()` |
| any | `census` | a reconciliation is due |
| `census` | `ready` | the census closed |
| any | `draining` | the dirty set is non-empty at the drain |
| any | `waiting` | no admissible candidate, and (since 0796) no backlog |
| any | *a reason* | `host.blocked()` returned one at candidate gating |
| any | *an admission reason* | `beforeSubmit` refused |
| any | `extracting` | a document was admitted |
| `extracting` | `waiting` | that document settled |
| any | `error` | the sweep threw |
| any | `switched-off` | `disarmSDTSitter` in `bootstrap.js` |

Two of these rows are the whole problem in miniature: *a reason* and *an
admission reason* are not states, they are a diagnosis written into the state
variable, and they come from a vocabulary the scheduler does not own.

## The objection this ticket must answer, because a decorrelated review raised it

A Fable review of the wider re-architecture argued a transition graph is not
writable here: the scheduler would have to learn `bootstrap.js`'s `blocked()`
vocabulary, which is the layering the codebase keeps separate on purpose —
`SDT_STATUS_CLASSES`'s own comment says its class is "deliberately NOT
`blocked`" because `host.blocked()` answers a different question.

The answer this ticket proposes, and which the implementer must defend or
abandon rather than assume: with the alphabet declared in **one place both
sides read**, the scheduler is not learning the host's words — the two are
reading a shared declaration. That is a different arrangement from the one the
review refuted. If, on contact with the code, it turns out the host must be
free to invent reasons at runtime, then the honest outcome is a **partial**
relation: edges declared for the closed part, an explicitly wildcarded edge for
reasons, and the wildcard named as a gap rather than passed off as coverage.

## Decided: requested everywhere, applied at once, checked at the centre

The author asked whether transitions should happen only at a tick or be
requested from everywhere. It is decided here rather than left open, because an
implementer who meets the question cold will settle it the other way.

**A tick-only model requires the state to be observable, and half of this state
is not.** `extracting` is true because we called `ensure()`; `census` because
control is inside that loop. Those are facts about where the program is, not
about the world, and nothing outside the control flow can rediscover them. The
positional phases must be requested from the site that knows the position,
because no other site does.

**The assignment also carries control flow today**, which is where a deferred
model breaks. `state.phase = admissionReason; publish(); break;` is one gesture:
the phase and the decision to leave the loop. Defer the application to a tick
and the decision to leave must still be taken on the spot — two mechanisms
where there was one, and the second is a new assignment by another name.

**What a tick would buy is already bought.** Its real prize is one place where
a transition actually happens, hence one place where the relation is checked.
`setPhase` gives exactly that, without a tick's latency, without flapping, and
without requiring the world to be re-observable. The defect was never that
transitions happened at the wrong moment; it was that they were checked
nowhere.

So: **sites keep the initiative, because only they know; `setPhase` keeps the
refusal, because only it knows the alphabet and the relation.**

One honest reservation, and it is the only place the question bites. The reason
axis IS observable, and while the sitter sits on `low-disk` nothing re-examines
it until the next sweep — a tick would notice the disk freed. But that tick
exists: it is the sweep, and `nextSweepDelayMS` schedules it for precisely this.
A second cadence would cost the periodic-cost-while-idle that 0795's invariants
and 0701 both forbid. If an implementer believes the sweep's cadence is too
coarse for some reason, that is a change to `nextSweepDelayMS`, not a new
clock.

## What this ticket does NOT claim

The same review demolished two payoffs that were originally attached to the
funnel, and they are not resurrected here:

- **`phaseSince` would lie in the case that matters.** The abort path
  deliberately leaves `phase` stale at `extracting`, so an age computed at
  assignment reports the age of a phase the machine has left. And the journal
  already records `phase` with `elapsedMS` every sixty seconds — both incident
  files of 2026-09-15 were reconstructed from exactly that. A `phaseSince`
  field is a second copy of a fact the journal owns.
- **No tick re-derivation.** Half the reason axis is per-candidate —
  `storage-unavailable` and `low-disk` read the candidate's own directory — so
  a tick with no candidate cannot compute it without guessing.

### 4. Propagate the model to the design doc — FIRST, not last

**Reversed by the author on 2026-09-16**: "design doc en premier, c'est lui
qu'on veut reviewer d'abord." An earlier revision of this ticket said the model
must not reach `DESIGN-NOTES.md` before the code, on the ground that the
document would describe a plugin that does not exist. That rule is not
abandoned, it is satisfied differently: the section is written as a **design
under review**, marked as such, so no reader can mistake it for shipped
behaviour. The model is what he wants to argue with; an implementation reviewed
in its place would be arguing about the model through its consequences.

This is child ticket 0803, and it gates the rest.

## Relevant files

- `plugins/sdt-sitter/scheduler.js` — the ten assignment sites in `pump()` and
  the sweep's error path; cite by symbol.
- `plugins/sdt-sitter/bootstrap.js` — `SDT_PHASE_LABELS`, `SDT_BLOCKED_PHASES`,
  `blocked()`, `nextSweepDelayMS`, `describeSDTSwitchLine`,
  `describeSDTSwitchKind`, `describeSDTTooltip`, `getSDTCoverage` (reads
  `ready`), `toggleSDTSwitch` (reads `switched-off`), `disarmSDTSitter`.
- `tests/sdt_sitter_scheduler.mjs` — **the regex phase discovery and its
  `phases.size >= 12` floor.** A funnel silently empties two of its three
  extractors; the floor is the only thing that would turn that into a failure,
  and it must be re-verified with a positive control before it is trusted.
- `verification/probes/sdt_sitter_scheduler_mutants.py` — mutants anchored on
  the exact assignment text. Establish whether an unmatched anchor fails the
  probe or passes vacuously; if it passes vacuously, that is a finding in its
  own right.
- `tests/test_sdt_sitter.py` — parses the `SDT_PHASE_LABELS` literal as JSON, so
  a table keyed by axis breaks the parser.
- `tests/sdt_sitter_bootstrap.mjs`, `tests/sdt_sitter_dialog.mjs` — fixtures
  that set `phase` directly.

## Two one-way doors

- **The journal schema — RULED, 2026-09-16: break and migrate.** The author
  chose separate fields plus a reader that maps old records onto the new shape,
  over the cheaper alternative of serialising both axes back into one string on
  the wire. The more honest structure and the more expensive one; the
  serialising option is rejected and must not return as a simplification. Child
  ticket 0805 owns it, and its control is that the reader reproduces the two
  preserved 2026-09-15 incidents from their original JSON.
- **`Zotero.SDTPackSitter.state`** is the console surface the author's own live
  readings went through. Its shape is an external contract now.

## Test

Red first, and the alphabet makes property tests possible where only example
tests existed:

- For **every** phase in the declared table: its label id resolves, its cadence
  class is defined, and its axis is one of the declared axes. Today this cannot
  be written, because there is no table to iterate.
- `SDT_BLOCKED_PHASES` and `SDT_PHASE_LABELS` each equal their projection of the
  alphabet — which is the assertion that would have caught the
  `native-worker-busy` misfiling.
- `setPhase` refuses a value outside the alphabet and emits the defect record.
  The control: feed it a plausible-looking unknown reason and assert the record,
  not merely that nothing crashed.
- One declared transition and one undeclared one, asserted in both directions.

Every threshold or set boundary introduced gets an assertion on its exact value
and one a unit off, and the mutant is **run**, not assumed.

## Invariants

- No behaviour change in what the sitter does. This is an audit surface, not a
  policy change.
- `ready` and `waiting` stay distinct: `getSDTCoverage` computes `known` from
  `phase !== 'ready'`, and `ready` is deliberately silent to absorb the
  per-document admission gap.
- 0796's drain budget, 0795's phase and 0797's table keep working unchanged.
- No new periodic cost while idle.

## Decomposition — this ticket is a tracker

Ruled 2026-09-16: the blast radius is past the threshold (ten-plus files
including the test enumeration, the mutant anchors, the journal schema and an
external console contract), so this becomes a tracker and the work lands as
children. It stays open until all four are closed, and closes on the
integration review, never on the bare event of the last child merging.

- **0803** — write the model into `DESIGN-NOTES.md` for review. **First**, and
  it gates the others. No code.
- **0804** — declare the alphabet, make `setPhase` total. `Blocked-by: 0803`.
- **0805** — the journal's three fields and the reader for old records.
  `Blocked-by: 0804`. Goes through the one-way door above.
- **0806** — the intended, static and dynamic graphs, and the ruling on each of
  their three differences. `Blocked-by: 0804`. This is the funnel's surviving
  dividend.

Ticket **0802** (the phase enumeration counts a literal inside a comment) is
the narrow repair of the same scrape 0804 removes: if 0804 lands first, close
0802 as superseded rather than implementing it.

## Exit criteria

The alphabet is declared in one place; `SDT_PHASE_LABELS` and
`SDT_BLOCKED_PHASES` are projections of it rather than parallel lists; every
assignment passes a check that refuses an unknown value and reports it; and a
test iterates the declaration rather than scraping the source for it.

And the transition work is finished when **three graphs exist and every gap
between them has been ruled on** — not when the transitions are "declared".

1. **The intended graph**, declared by hand with its conditions. A
   specification.
2. **The static graph**, extracted from the code: which `setPhase(to)` calls
   exist and from which predecessors they are reachable. What the code *can*
   express.
3. **The dynamic graph**, observed: instrument `setPhase`, run the full suite,
   record every `(from → to)` actually taken. What the code *does*, wherever
   the tests reach.

The three differences each mean something different, and each must be ruled on
rather than listed:

- **In the code, absent from the spec** — undocumented behaviour. Fix it or
  write it down; never leave it.
- **In the spec, absent from the code** — unimplemented, or dead specification.
- **Static but never dynamic** — untested *or* unreachable, and **the ticket
  must say which**. A zero of observation cannot tell those apart, which is
  this project's own positive-control rule applied to a graph.

The graph ships as a **checked-in artifact compared by a test**, not as prose:
a drift then reddens instead of rotting, which is the failure the line-number
citations demonstrated three times on 2026-09-15. It is generated, so it is
regenerated rather than hand-merged on a conflict.

Note that the dynamic extraction is the funnel's real second dividend. The
first one originally claimed, `phaseSince`, did not survive review; this one
does, and a single passage point is the only place it can be obtained without
rewriting the code under instrumentation.
